  "generated_text": "Given that \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are inversely proportional, we can write the relationship as:\n\\[ p \\cdot q = k \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is a constant.\n\nWe are given that \\( p = 25 \\) when \\( q = 6 \\). Substituting these values into the equation, we get:\n\\[ 25 \\cdot 6 = k \\]\n\\[ 150 = k \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value of \\( p \\) when \\( q = 15 \\). Using the relationship \\( p \\cdot q = k \\) and substituting \\( q = 15 \\) and \\( k = 150 \\), we get:\n\\[ p \\cdot 15 = 150 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( p \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 15:\n\\[ p = \\frac{150}{15} \\]\n\\[ p = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( p \\) when \\( q = 15 \\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[10, '10']",
    "gold_answer": "If $p$ and $q$ are inversely proportional, then $p\\cdot{q}=k$ (where $k$ is a constant). We know that $p=25$ when $q=6$, so $(25)(6)=k$ or $k=150$. Thus when $q=15$, $(p)(15)=150$ and $p=\\boxed{10}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
